---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Support and Resistance Explained";
const description =
  "What support and resistance actually are, why round numbers and prior highs " +
  "matter, and why a level is a zone rather than a line.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Technical analysis / Support and resistance">
  <h1>Support and resistance</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A support level is a price where buying has previously been enough to stop a
    decline. Resistance is where selling has previously been enough to stop an
    advance. Both are observations about what has already happened, not
    predictions about what will.
  </p>

  <h2>Why levels exist at all</h2>
  <p>
    The mechanism is ordinary human behaviour rather than anything mystical:
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>memory_of_a_price</code><span class="meta">the main driver</span></div>
      <p>People who bought near a previous high and then watched it fall often
        sell when price returns to what they paid. That supply is why old highs
        act as resistance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>round_numbers</code><span class="meta">self-fulfilling</span></div>
      <p>Orders cluster at ₹100, ₹500, ₹1,000. There is nothing special about
        those prices except that everyone uses them, which is enough.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>widely_watched_averages</code><span class="meta">also self-fulfilling</span></div>
      <p>The 200-day average works partly because a great many people are watching
        it and acting near it. See
        <a href="/learn/moving-averages/">moving averages</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>A level is a zone, not a line</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Drawing a support line at exactly ₹1,247.30 implies a precision that does not
    exist. What actually happens is that buying appears somewhere in a band — and
    the band widens with volatility. On a stock with an ATR of 3% of price, a
    "level" is realistically a few percent wide.
  </p>
  <p>
    That matters directly for stop placement: a stop sitting just below a
    well-known level is in the exact region where price probes before turning.
    See <a href="/tools/position-sizing/">position sizing</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The role reversal</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The commonly taught idea is that broken resistance becomes support, and
    broken support becomes resistance. The reasoning is that buyers who missed
    the move wait to buy a retest, while those who sold too early buy back.
  </p>
  <p>
    It happens often enough to be worth knowing and fails often enough that it is
    not a rule. Levels break decisively and never look back all the time; the
    examples used to teach the concept are chosen after the fact from the cases
    where it worked.
  </p>

  <h2>What the data can and cannot tell you</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>52_week_high</code><span class="meta">on this site</span></div>
      <p>The clearest objective resistance reference, because it needs no
        judgement. The <a href="/screener/">screener</a> publishes distance from
        it as <span class="c">pct_from_52wh</span>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>prior_pivot_lows</code><span class="meta">objective, if defined carefully</span></div>
      <p>A swing low — a bar lower than the several bars either side — can be
        identified mechanically. What cannot be mechanised is which of the many
        such lows anyone actually cares about.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>hand_drawn_lines</code><span class="meta">not published here</span></div>
      <p>Trendlines and channels depend on which points you choose to connect,
        and different analysts draw different lines on the same chart. This site
        does not publish them, because there is no objective version.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The honest caveat</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Support and resistance is the part of technical analysis most vulnerable to
    hindsight. On any completed chart you can find levels that "held" — because
    you are looking at where price turned and then calling it a level. The test
    is whether you would have drawn the same line <em>before</em> the turn, and
    on most charts the answer is no.
  </p>
  <p>
    Treat levels as a description of where transactions have previously clustered,
    which is genuinely useful context. Treat them as forecasts and you are fitting
    a story to a chart you have already seen. See
    <a href="/learn/overfitting/">overfitting</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/moving-averages/">moving averages</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/rsi/">RSI</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/risk-management/">risk management</a>
  </p>
</Base>
